\subsubsection{Claim 3: CPF-guided observed-support regularization improves biased-view robustness}

Results across all three biased viewpoint settings support our third claim that CPF-guided observed-support regularization provides effective guidance for 3DGS optimization. For example, under systematic coverage gaps (Table~\ref{tab:coverage_gap}), our method achieves the best or highly competitive performance across gap sizes and metrics on both Mip-NeRF 360~\cite{mipnerf360} and Tanks \& Temples~\cite{tankstemples}. Fig.~\ref{coverage_gap_trend} further shows that our method maintains a clear advantage as the coverage gap becomes more severe. Under discontinuous trajectories (Table~\ref{tab:discontinuous}), our method obtains the best average performance across all three datasets. Under viewing angle disparities (Table~\ref{tab:angle_disparity}), our method remains competitive with or better than the strongest stochastic baselines, especially on PSNR and LPIPS.

The performance rankings further support the design of GS-LSS. The improvement over deterministic-only methods, such as 3DGS~\cite{3dgs} and Scaffold-GS~\cite{scaffold}, supports the view that photometric optimization alone is insufficient under biased viewpoints. The improvement over geometric methods, such as Hierarchical-GS~\cite{hierarchical}, MVGS~\cite{mvgs} and PGSR~\cite{pgsr}, suggests that observation-driven regularization alone may not provide sufficient support guidance under biased viewpoints. 
The improvement over MCMC-based methods, such as 3DGS-MCMC~\cite{mcmc} and MH-3DGS~\cite{mh3dgs}, indicates that stochasticity alone provides limited global exploration. 
Instead, CPF-guided observed-support regularization provides explicit guidance for 3DGS optimization, effectively mitigating perspective overfitting and improving novel view synthesis under various biased viewpoint scenarios. 

