\section{Limitations}

GS-LSS regularizes Gaussian primitives toward aggregated observed support, but it does not infer fully unobserved geometry. When the available views provide no reliable support for a region, CPF can suppress unsupported drift and reduce view-specific artifacts, but it cannot synthesize missing scene details with guaranteed correctness. Since CPF supervision is derived from in-training 3DGS geometry, severely corrupted depth, dynamic objects, specular surfaces, or very sparse initial geometry may introduce inaccurate support estimates. The DBSCAN filtering, normal smoothness, delayed CPF supervision, and observation-aware gate are designed to reduce this risk, but they cannot remove it completely. In addition, CPF training and per-primitive LSS updates introduce additional computation compared with vanilla 3DGS. Future work will improve the reliability of support estimation and reduce the optimization overhead.

\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we present GS-LSS, an observed-support regularized learned stochastic optimization framework for Gaussian Splatting under biased viewpoints. Rather than relying solely on view-dependent photometric supervision, our approach aligns Gaussian primitives with an aggregated observed-support field through a per-primitive Learned Stochastic Sampling process.
The core of our method is a Continuous Probability Field network that estimates observed support from globally aggregated geometric priors and provides explicit guidance through gradient-based directional drift together with adaptive stochastic exploration. 
Experiments on three representative biased viewpoint scenarios show that our method achieves strong quantitative and qualitative results among the evaluated baselines, mitigating perspective overfitting and improving observed-support consistency at a moderate training overhead.
We believe that CPF-guided LSS extends the robustness of 3DGS to more challenging real-world acquisition settings, and in future work we will focus on improving the efficiency and reliability of the support estimation while maintaining high-quality rendering. 
